{# The four things somebody checks before opening a search, then the shape of
   the trip.

   The price leads, because it is the one figure the visitor came for and the
   only one that moves. It is a "from" price and reads as one: the cheapest
   single economy seat on any upcoming date, which is what the strip below
   shows the whole of.

   The time in the air is the average and not the fastest -- see
   RouteRepository::summary(). The distance is the shortest of the airport pairs
   on the route, which is the one that is a fact about the geography rather than
   about which airport a carrier happens to use. #}
{% include 'partials/facts-grid.html.twig' with {
    'facts': [
        {
            'icon': 'fa-tag',
            'value': '$' ~ place.cheapest|round(0, 'floor'),
            'label': 'Cheapest fare'
        },
        { 'icon': 'fa-clock', 'value': duration(place.typical), 'label': 'Time in the air' },
        { 'icon': 'fa-ruler', 'value': place.km|number_format ~ ' km', 'label': 'Distance' },
        {
            'icon': 'fa-plane',
            'value': place.carriers,
            'label': place.carriers > 1 ? 'Airlines' : 'Airline'
        }
    ]
} only %}

{# Both ends on one map, with the flight drawn between them.

   The line is a great circle and not a straight one, because on the projection
   a map draws, straight is the wrong shape: New York to London leaves the coast
   heading north-east over Newfoundland, and a straight line sends it across the
   middle of the Atlantic. GreatCircle works out the points; a route crossing
   the antimeridian comes back as two runs, drawn as two lines, which is why
   this joins them with "~" rather than assuming one.

   Marked at each city's own centre -- the mean of its airports -- which is what
   CityRepository::byCode() returns. Yandex frames two points itself, so no z is
   given. #}
{% set lines = [] %}
{% for run in place.path %}
    {% set coordinates = [] %}
    {% for point in run %}
        {% set coordinates = coordinates|merge([point.lon ~ ',' ~ point.lat]) %}
    {% endfor %}
    {# The site's own accent, which reads on both the water and the land. #}
    {% set lines = lines|merge(['c:0F766EFF,w:5,' ~ coordinates|join(',')]) %}
{% endfor %}

{# The pins carry no number. A "1" and a "2" on a map of two places name
   neither of them, and this service cannot letter a pin: text where the number
   goes is rejected outright, and its `text` parameter is accepted and then
   ignored. Both checked against it.

   So the two ends are told apart by colour -- green for where you leave, red
   for where you land, which is the one convention nobody has to be taught --
   and the legend under the map is what says which city each is. #}
<img class="place__map"
     src="https://static-maps.yandex.ru/1.x/?l=map&lang=en-US&size=650,300&pt={{ place.from.longitude }},{{ place.from.latitude }},pm2gnm~{{ place.to.longitude }},{{ place.to.latitude }},pm2rdm&pl={{ lines|join('~') }}"
     width="650" height="300" loading="lazy"
     alt="Map of the route from {{ place.from.name }} to {{ place.to.name }}, with the flight path drawn between them">

{# Read as text rather than only seen as colour, so the map is described for
   somebody who cannot make out the two pins -- or for whom the image never
   arrives. #}
<ul class="place__legend">
    <li class="place__legend-item place__legend-item--from">
        <span class="place__legend-role">From</span> {{ place.from.name }}
    </li>
    <li class="place__legend-item place__legend-item--to">
        <span class="place__legend-role">To</span> {{ place.to.name }}
    </li>
</ul>
